\begin{proposition}[the phases and the two periods]\hypertarget{tag-000J}{}\label{ari:C.1.2}
\StatementRecord{ari:C.1.2}{proved}{000J}{\SeriesRef{ari:C.1.1}}

With the branch continued from the specified cusp, the exact values are
\begin{align*}
 \frac{\vartheta _1((2-\rho)/3,\rho)}{\eta(\rho)}
   &=e^{\pi i/12}\,3^{1/4}e^{\pi/(6\sqrt3)},\\
 \frac{\vartheta _1((1-i)/2,i)}{\eta(i)}
   &=\sqrt2\,e^{\pi/4},\\
 \beta^{\rm part}(z_1)
   &=-\frac12+i\left(\frac1{\sqrt3}-\frac3{2\pi}\log48\right),\\
 \beta^{\rm part}(z_2)
   &=i\left(\frac32-\frac3\pi\log6\right).
\end{align*}
Consequently $N(z_1)=3^{1/4}$, $N(z_2)=\sqrt2$,
$D_{\rm part}(z_1)=-3\log48/(2\pi)$, and
$D_{\rm part}(z_2)=-3\log6/\pi$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Local freeness and the determinant line]\hypertarget{tag-01RR}{}\label{inv:A1.1}
\StatementRecord{inv:A1.1}{proved}{01RR}{the definitions and constructions stated in the proof}

Both $\mathcal A$ and $\mathcal E$ are locally free, of ranks three and
two.  The vertical vector $\xi=\partial/\partial\zeta_2$ is nowhere
zero as a section of $\mathcal E^\vee$.  There is an exact sequence
of holomorphic vector bundles
\begin{equation}\hypertarget{tag-01SZ}{}\label{inv:eq:A-E-line}
0\longrightarrow N\longrightarrow\mathcal E
 \xrightarrow{\langle\cdot,\xi\rangle}\OO_X\longrightarrow0,
\qquad N=\det\mathcal E=\omega_f(-2S_1-3S_2).
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Identification of the elliptic logarithm and the verified zero formula]\hypertarget{tag-01RW}{}\label{inv:A2.3}
\StatementRecord{inv:A2.3}{proved}{01RW}{\SeriesRef{inv:eq:A-s-branch}, \SeriesRef{inv:eq:A-mu-value}; \citet{EichlerZagier1982}; \citet{DukeImamoglu2008}}

For the Weierstrass equation
\[
y^2=x^3-3t^3(t-1)x+2t^4(t-1)^2,
\]
the section $[\mu]$ is a generator of its rational section group and
equals one of $P_*=(0,\sqrt2t^2(t-1))$ and $-P_*$.
In particular $\wp(\mu,\tau)=0$ on every regular fibre.
Along the branch in \eqref{inv:eq:A-s-branch}, with
$x_0=1-1/t$, the exact branch is
\begin{equation}\hypertarget{tag-01SY}{}\label{inv:eq:A-DI-branch}
\mu=\frac{1-\tau}{2}
 +\frac{i\sqrt6}{3\pi}
 \frac{x_0^{1/4}\,{}_3F_2(1/3,2/3,1;3/4,5/4;x_0)}
 {{}_2F_1(1/12,5/12;1;1-x_0)}.
\end{equation}
Consequently $\mu-(2-\tau)/3$ has exact order two at the
order-three point in $r^3=t$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Refuted: The claimed degree-one torsion along the cusp]\hypertarget{tag-01S1}{}\label{inv:A4.2}
\StatementRecord{inv:A4.2}{proved}{01S1}{\SeriesRef{inv:A4.1}}

The assertion in AC26 Remark 9.21, PDF page 81, that
$\Omega_{X/B}^1$ has torsion along $W\cup S_1\cup S_2$ is false.
Its torsion is supported precisely on $S_1\cup S_2$; its degree-one
cusp modules are torsion-free but not locally free on the double
locus.  The plural statement in Remark 1.3, PDF page 5, must be
qualified by degree: $\Omega^2_{X/B}$ does have torsion on the cusp
double locus.  The workbench's degree-one correction is correct;
it must not be extended to degree two.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[The cusp length and the generic twist]\hypertarget{tag-01SP}{}\label{inv:D4.2}
\StatementRecord{inv:D4.2}{proved}{01SP}{\SeriesRef{inv:C3.2}, \SeriesRef{inv:D4.3}, \SeriesRef{inv:D1.2}}

Use the coordinate $M_z=\exp(z\mathfrak g)$ of \ref{inv:C3.2}. For every
$z\notin\Z$ the cusp contribution is exactly
\[
 (R^0f_*(T_X\otimes M_z))_{p_0}=0,\quad
 (R^1f_*(T_X\otimes M_z))_{p_0}=0,\quad
 (R^2f_*(T_X\otimes M_z))_{p_0}=\C\{q\}/(q).           \tag{D.14}
\]
Outside the countable exceptional set explicitly defined in \ref{inv:D4.3},
these are the only nonzero direct-image stalks. Hence the generic
twist has $(h^0,h^1,h^2,h^3)=(0,0,1,0)$, and the cusp nilpotence exponent $r$ equals one there.
\end{proposition}
